## What the analysis found

- **The original confidence interval was too narrow.** Visitors average {{ "%.2f" | format(r.sessions_per_visitor) }} sessions, and clustering by visitor widens the SE by {{ "%.2f" | format(r.se_inflation_from_clustering) }}x.
- **Adjusting for measured confounders barely moves the gap.** AIPW uses only session-start covariates (time, geography, visit history); in-session behaviour is downstream of device and was never pulled. Overlap is {{ "adequate" if r.overlap_set_A.adequate else "INADEQUATE" }} and weighting cuts max |SMD| from {{ "%.3f" | format(r.balance_set_A.max_abs_smd_before) }} to {{ "%.3f" | format(r.balance_set_A.max_abs_smd_after) }}.
- **One segment drives much of the gap.** "Referral" traffic with source "(direct)" has {{ "{:,}".format(rd.n_desktop) }} desktop sessions, {{ "{:,}".format(rd.n_mobile) }} mobile ones, and converts at {{ rd.conversion_rate_desktop | pct(1) }} on desktop. The excluded traffic holds {{ r.internal_traffic.share_of_desktop_conversions | pct(1) }} of desktop conversions. It looks like internal purchasing, but that can't be confirmed, and the exclusion is post-hoc, so the full-data estimate stays primary.
- **A large E-value does not make the causal reading safe.** A confounder would need a risk-ratio association of {{ "%.2f" | format(r.e_value.e_value_point) }} with both device and conversion to explain the gap away. Purchase intent plausibly clears that bar with conversion. Cross-device journeys (browse on phone, buy on desktop) are invisible here and alone could produce the gap.

## What to do next

Run a randomized test of a **specific** mobile change (e.g. checkout), randomized and analysed by visitor. {% set p25 = r.future_test_power.rows | selectattr("relative_mde", "equalto", 0.25) | first %}At a {{ r.future_test_power.baseline_mobile_rate | pct(2) }} mobile conversion rate, detecting a 25% lift needs about {{ "{:,}".format(p25.n_per_arm) }} sessions per arm, or {{ "%.1f" | format(p25.months_of_mobile_traffic) }} months of mobile traffic, before any visitor-clustering design effect. So test a bold change, use a higher-frequency metric such as add-to-cart, or use CUPED on pre-period activity.
